\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
C3PO, a ray-tracing code for arbitrary axisymmetric magnetic equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Yves Peysson and Joan Decker 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
09/01/2008
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset toc
LatexCommand tableofcontents

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to calculate power and current deposition from RF waves in plasmas,
 it is necessary to determine the wave characteristics inside the plasma.
 In a quasi-stationary plasma where the equilibrium varies on a scale that
 is much larger than the wavelength, it is possible to calculate the wave
 propagation from antenna or mirror conditions and equilibrium properties
 using the ray-tracing techniques.
 This method has been used extensively for calculating lower-hybrid and
 electron cyclotron wave propagation 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "bon84,biz93,pey96,imb05"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Many ray tracing codes in axisymmetric toroidal plasmas use the toroidal
 coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $(R,Z,\phi)$
\end_inset

 which are attractive because of the simplicity of implementing the equations
 in this system 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "mat89,smi95,smi03,imb05"

\end_inset

.
 In this work we use the flux coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rho,\theta,\phi)$
\end_inset

 instead.
 While the equations in this system require somewhat more complicated metric
 coefficients, there are several advantages to this choice: first, any equilibri
um property that is a flux function, such as the density and temperature
 - or more generally the distribution function -, depends upon 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 only; second, the periodicity of the coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 allows the use of Fourier series that are particularly useful for accurate
 interpolation and the calculation of derivatives up to second order; third,
 this system is used by a variety of codes that are coupled to the ray-tracing
 solver, such as equilibrium codes (HELENA) and Fokker-Planck solvers (LUKE)
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "huy91,dec04a"

\end_inset

.
 Using the same system in the ray-tracing code saves any coordinates conversions
 and grid interpolations which introduce numerical errors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The new ray-tracing code developed here is designed for arbitrary wave frequenci
es (including the lower hybrid and electron cyclotron frequency ranges),
 provided the WKB approximation is valid.
 The structure of the code allows to choose from several dielectric tensor
 types: cold plasma, "warm" plasma 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "bon86"

\end_inset

, and kinetic plasma in the non-, weakly-, or fully relativistic regime.
 In addition to ray trajectories, the code calculates waves properties of
 interest for heating and current drive calculations such as the polarization,
 energy flow and linear absorption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Based on a standard 
\begin_inset Formula $6^{th}$
\end_inset

 order Runge-Kutta differential scheme 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pre92"

\end_inset

, the ray-tracing code uses a spline-Fourier expansion for equilibrium interpola
tion, which results in fast yet accurate calculations.
 The code is distributed as a mexfile
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The code runs in the MatLab 7 programming environment.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 written in 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 language.
 It is benchmarked extensively for various types of waves against both existing
 codes and analytical models.
 In Sec.
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Linear-wave-theory"

\end_inset

, the linear wave theory in an infinite uniform medium is presented.
 Ray-tracing in slowly varying plasma is derived in Sec.
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Ray-tracing-in"

\end_inset

.
 The curvilinear canonical flux coordinates in an axisymmetric toroidal
 plasma are introduced in Sec.
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Canonical-coordinates-in"

\end_inset

.
 In Sec.
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Numerical-algorithms"

\end_inset

 numerical methods are presented.
 A conclusion is given in Sec.
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Conclusion"

\end_inset

.
 The Appendix includes some properties of the coordinate system (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Explicit-expressions"

\end_inset

), explicit expressions for the dispersion relations and some derivatives
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Various-properties-of"

\end_inset

), the cold and hot plasma dielectric tensors (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Susceptibility-tensor-and"

\end_inset

,
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Kinetic-plasma-model"

\end_inset

), and the benchmarking results (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Benchmarking-of-C3PO"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Linear-wave-theory"

\end_inset

Linear wave theory in an infinite uniform plasma
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Maxwell's equations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Electromagnetic fields are generated by charge and current densities according
 to Maxwell's equations
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{E}=\dfrac{\rho}{\varepsilon_{0}}\\
\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{B}=0\\
\mathbf{\nabla}\times\mathbf{E}=-\dfrac{\partial\mathbf{B}}{\partial t}\\
\mathbf{\nabla}\times\mathbf{B}=\mu_{0}\mathbf{J}+\varepsilon_{0}\mu_{0}\dfrac{\partial\mathbf{E}}{\partial t}\end{array}\label{maxwell}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{E}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)$
\end_inset

 are the electric and magnetic fields, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{J}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)$
\end_inset

 are the charge and current densities.
 Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}$
\end_inset

 is the vector position, and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is the time.
 The continuity equation - or charge conservation - relating the charge
 density 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)$
\end_inset

 to the current density 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{J}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)$
\end_inset

 is obtained from (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "maxwell"

\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\rho}{\partial t}+\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{J}=0\label{continuity}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Constitutive relation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Maxwell equations (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "maxwell"

\end_inset

) are a closed system only if the charge and the current densities are known.
 In a plasma 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{J}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)$
\end_inset

 are functions of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{E}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)$
\end_inset

.
 The expression of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{J}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)$
\end_inset

 as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{E}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)$
\end_inset

 is called the constitutive relation.
 For linearized fields, this relation can be expressed in general as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{J}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)=\iiint d^{3}\mathbf{X}^{\prime}\int dt^{\prime}\;\mathbb{S}\left(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{X}^{\prime},t,t^{\prime}\right)\cdot\mathbf{E}\left(\mathbf{X}^{\prime},t^{\prime}\right)\label{constitutive}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{S}\left(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{X}^{\prime},t,t^{\prime}\right)$
\end_inset

 is the conductivity tensor, which depends upon the plasma equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Fourier transform
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a infinite plasma with an homogeneous constant equilibrium that is invariant
 by translation in space and time, the conductivity tensor is only a function
 of the relative distance in space and time, and Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "constitutive"

\end_inset

 may be rewritten as a convolution
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{J}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)=\iiint d^{3}\mathbf{X}^{\prime}\int dt^{\prime}\;\mathbb{S}\left(\mathbf{X}-\mathbf{X}^{\prime},t-t^{\prime}\right)\cdot\mathbf{E}\left(\mathbf{X}^{\prime},t^{\prime}\right)\label{constitutive2}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Applying the Fourier transform to the fields yields
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\mathbf{E}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)={\displaystyle \int}d\omega{\displaystyle \iiint}d^{3}\mathbf{k\; E}_{\mathbf{k}}\left(\mathbf{k},\omega\right)e^{i\left(\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{X}-\omega t\right)}\\
\mathbf{J}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)={\displaystyle \int}d\omega{\displaystyle \iiint}d^{3}\mathbf{k\; J}_{\mathbf{k}}\left(\mathbf{k},\omega\right)e^{i\left(\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{X}-\omega t\right)}\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 with the reverse relations
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}\left(\mathbf{k},\omega\right)=\dfrac{1}{\left(2\pi\right)^{4}}{\displaystyle \iiint}d^{3}\mathbf{X}{\displaystyle \int}dt\;\mathbf{E}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)e^{-i\left(\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{X}-\omega t\right)}\\
\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{k}}\left(\mathbf{k},\omega\right)=\dfrac{1}{\left(2\pi\right)^{4}}{\displaystyle \iiint}d^{3}\mathbf{X}{\displaystyle \int}dt\;\mathbf{J}\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)e^{-i\left(\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{X}-\omega t\right)}\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 such that (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "constitutive2"

\end_inset

) becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{k}}\left(\mathbf{k},\omega\right)=\mathbb{S}\left(\mathbf{k},\omega\right)\cdot\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}\left(\mathbf{k},\omega\right)\label{constitutivefourier}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Similarly, applying the Fourier transform to the Maxwell's equations gives
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
i\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}=\dfrac{\rho_{\mathbf{k}}}{\varepsilon_{0}}\\
i\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{B}_{\mathbf{k}}=0\\
i\mathbf{k}\times\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}=i\omega\mathbf{B}_{\mathbf{k}}\\
i\mathbf{k}\times\mathbf{B}_{\mathbf{k}}=\mu_{0}\mathbf{J}_{\mathbf{k}}-\varepsilon_{0}\mu_{0}i\omega\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}\end{array}\label{maxwell2}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Linear wave equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Combining the last two equations in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "maxwell2"

\end_inset

) with (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "constitutivefourier"

\end_inset

) leads to the linear wave equation
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{k}\times\mathbf{k}\times\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}+\frac{\omega^{2}}{c^{2}}\mathbb{K}\left(\mathbf{k},\omega\right)\cdot\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}=0\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbb{K}\left(\mathbf{k},\omega\right)=\mathbb{I}+\mathbb{X}\left(\mathbf{k},\omega\right)\label{permittivity}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 is the permittivity tensor, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbb{X}\left(\mathbf{k},\omega\right)=\frac{i}{\varepsilon_{0}\omega}\mathbb{S}\left(\mathbf{k},\omega\right)\label{susceptibility}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 is the susceptibility tensor and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{I}$
\end_inset

 is the unit tensor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Introducing the index of refraction
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{N}\equiv\frac{c}{\omega}\mathbf{k}\label{indexrefraction}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 the wave equation becomes
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{N}\times\mathbf{N}\times\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}+\mathbb{K}\left(\mathbf{N},\omega\right)\cdot\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}=0\end{equation}

\end_inset

 or equivalently
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbb{D}\cdot\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}=0\label{maxwellwaveeq1}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{D}\left(\mathbf{N},\omega\right)$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbb{D}=\mathbf{NN}-N^{2}\mathbb{I}+\mathbb{K}\left(\mathbf{N},\omega\right)\label{Maxwelltensor}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 is the dispersion tensor.
 The susceptibility 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{X}$
\end_inset

 is the sum of contributions from all species
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbb{X}=\sum_{s}\mathbb{X}^{s}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 In the case where the distribution function of the species 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 is a Maxwellian, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{X}^{s}$
\end_inset

 depends upon the following non-dimensional parameters : the refractive
 index 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{N}$
\end_inset

, the thermal velocity normalized to the speed of light 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{Ts}=v_{Ts}/c$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{Ts}=\sqrt{kT_{s}/m_{s}}$
\end_inset

, and the ratios 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\omega}_{ps}=\omega_{ps}/\omega$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\omega}_{cs}=\omega_{cs}/\omega$
\end_inset

 of the plasma frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{ps}=\sqrt{q_{s}^{2}n_{s}/\varepsilon_{0}m_{s}}$
\end_inset

 and the cyclotron frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{cs}=q_{s}B_{0}/m_{s}$
\end_inset

 to the wave frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore the dispersion tensor may be expressed in the general form
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbb{D=}\mathbf{NN-N}^{2}\mathbb{I}+\mathbb{K}\left(\mathbf{N},\beta_{Ts},\overline{\omega}_{ps},\overline{\omega}_{cs}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Dispersion relation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to obtain non-trivial solutions to the Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "maxwellwaveeq1"

\end_inset

, the determinant of the dispersion tensor must be zero, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}\left(\mathbf{N},\beta_{Ts},\overline{\omega}_{ps},\overline{\omega}_{cs}\right)\equiv\det\mathbb{D}\left(\mathbf{N},\beta_{Ts},\overline{\omega}_{ps},\overline{\omega}_{cs}\right)=0\label{disprel}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 which defines the dispersion relation.
 This relation gives the local electromagnetic eigenmodes that can be excited
 independently in the homogenous plasma.
 By cylindrical symmetry about the direction of the magnetic field 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\hat{b}}=\mathbf{B}_{0}/B_{0}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}$
\end_inset

 depends only upon the components of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{N}$
\end_inset

 parallel and perpendicular to the magnetic field, defined respectively
 as 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\Vert}=\mathbf{N}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{b}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\bot}=\left\Vert \mathbf{N}\times\mathbf{\hat{b}}\right\Vert $
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the dispersion relation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "disprel"

\end_inset

) can be solved for either 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega\left(N_{\Vert},N_{\bot}\right)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\Vert}\left(\omega,N_{\bot}\right)$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\bot}\left(\omega,N_{\Vert}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 The polarization of any eigenmode resulting from equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "disprel"

\end_inset

) is determined by the corresponding eigenvector of the wave equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "maxwellwaveeq1"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Explicit expressions of the dispersion relation are given in Appendix A1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Weak damping approximation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\Vert}$
\end_inset

 are given real quantities, (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "disprel"

\end_inset

) generally leads to a complex 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\bot}=N_{\bot r}+iN_{\bot i}$
\end_inset

.
 Meanwhile, the dispersion tensor 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{D}$
\end_inset

 can be generally decomposed as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{D}=\mathbb{D}^{\text{H}}+i\mathbb{D}^{\text{A}}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{D}^{\text{H}}=(\mathbb{D}+\mathbb{D}^{\dag})/2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{D}^{\text{A}}=(\mathbb{D}-\mathbb{D}^{\dag})/2i$
\end_inset

 are the hermitian and antihermitian parts of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{D}$
\end_inset

, respectively.
 In this work, the weak damping approximation is considered, which is valid
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $|D_{ij}^{\text{A}}|\ll|D_{ij}^{\text{H}}|$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset space \space{}
\end_inset

In that case, it can be shown 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "dec06"

\end_inset

 that 
\begin_inset Formula $|N_{\bot i}|\ll|N_{\bot r}|$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\bot r}$
\end_inset

 can be determined from solving the approximate wave equation
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbb{D}^{\text{H}}\left(N_{\bot r}\right)\cdot\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}=0\label{maxwellwaveeq2}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 with dispersion relation
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}^{\text{H}}\left(N_{\Vert},N_{\bot r},\beta_{Ts},\overline{\omega}_{ps},\overline{\omega}_{cs}\right)\equiv\det\mathbb{D}^{\text{H}}\left(N_{\Vert},N_{\bot r},\beta_{Ts},\overline{\omega}_{ps},\overline{\omega}_{cs}\right)=0\label{disprel3}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 In this approximation, the time-averaged densities of energy flow 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{S}$
\end_inset

 and dissipated power 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 can be obtained from 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\mathbf{S}=-\dfrac{\varepsilon_{0}c}{4}\dfrac{\partial}{\partial\mathbf{N}}\left(\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}^{\ast}\cdot\mathbb{D}^{\text{H}}\cdot\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}\right)\\
\\P=\dfrac{\varepsilon_{0}\omega}{2}\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}^{\ast}\cdot\mathbb{D}^{\text{A}}\cdot\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{k}}\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Note that the imaginary part of the wave vector can be calculated using
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
N_{\bot i}=\dfrac{c}{2\omega}\dfrac{P}{S_{\bot}}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 which yields the ray damping.
 From here on in this paper, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{N}$
\end_inset

 will refer to the zero-order, real solution of (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "disprel3"

\end_inset

), and the dispersion relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}$
\end_inset

 to the hermitian part 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}^{\text{H}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Ray-tracing-in"

\end_inset

Ray tracing in a slowly varying plasma
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
WKB Approximation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a slightly nonuniform plasma where 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\Vert \mathbf{k}\right\Vert \gg\dfrac{\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\beta_{Ts}\right\Vert }{\beta_{Ts}},\dfrac{\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\overline{\omega}_{ps}\right\Vert }{\overline{\omega}_{ps}},\dfrac{\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\overline{\omega}_{cs}\right\Vert }{\left\vert \overline{\omega}_{cs}\right\vert }\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and the wave characteristics also vary slowly 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\Vert \mathbf{k}\right\Vert ^{2}\gg\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}k\right\Vert \end{equation}

\end_inset

 the wave equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "maxwellwaveeq2"

\end_inset

) and dispersion relation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "disprel3"

\end_inset

) are still be satisfied locally, under the WKB approximation.
 The wave vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

 is then a slowly varying function of space and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}\left(\mathbf{X}\right)$
\end_inset

 can be determined using ray-tracing techniques.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Ray equations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ray equations in a plasma slowly varying in space and time are defined by
 the condition (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "disprel3"

\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}\left(\mathbf{X},t,\mathbf{k,}\omega\right)=0$
\end_inset

 along the ray trajectory.
 Therefore, at all points along the ray trajectory, the condition 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\delta\mathcal{D}=\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\mathbf{X}}\cdot\delta\mathbf{X}+\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial t}\delta t+\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\mathbf{k}}\cdot\delta\mathbf{k+}\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\omega}\delta\omega=0\label{deltaD}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 must be satisfied.
 Let define 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 a dimensionless parameter, which is a measure of distance along the trajectory.
 Then (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "deltaD"

\end_inset

) can be rewritten as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\delta\mathcal{D}=\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\mathbf{X}}\cdot\mathbf{\dot{X}}\delta\tau+\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial t}\dot{t}\delta\tau\mathbf{+}\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\mathbf{k}}\cdot\mathbf{\dot{k}}\delta\tau\mathbf{+}\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\omega}\dot{\omega}\delta\tau=0\label{deltaD2}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\dot{X}=}d\mathbf{X/}d\tau,$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\dot{k}=}d\mathbf{k/}d\tau,$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{t}\mathbf{=}dt\mathbf{/}d\tau$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{\omega}=d\omega\mathbf{/}d\tau.$
\end_inset

 The wave vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

 is expressed in coordinates that are canonically conjugate to those of
 the position vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\{ X_{i},k_{j}\right\} =\delta_{ij}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \ldots\right\} $
\end_inset

 is Poisson bracket, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is the Kronecker delta symbol, 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{j}$
\end_inset

 are the coordinate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

 respectively.
 The frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 and the time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 being also canonically conjugate, the relations
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\dfrac{d\mathbf{X}}{d\tau}=\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\mathbf{k}}\\
\\\dfrac{d\mathbf{k}}{d\tau}=-\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\mathbf{X}}\\
\\\dfrac{dt}{d\tau}=-\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\omega}\\
\\\dfrac{d\omega}{d\tau}=\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial t}\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 result from the Hamiltonian nature of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}$
\end_inset

, and Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "deltaD2"

\end_inset

 is automatically satisfied.
 These four equations are therefore the ray equations.
 From the first and the third equations, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{d\mathbf{X}}{dt}=\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\mathbf{k}}/\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\omega}=\frac{\partial\omega}{\partial\mathbf{k}}=\mathbf{v}_{g}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 meaning that the ray is directed along the group velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{g}$
\end_inset

 and thus indicates the direction of energy flow since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{g}\Vert\mathbf{S}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "bra98"

\end_inset

.
 In a constant plasma 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial\mathcal{D}/\partial t=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 is a constant of the ray dynamics.
 The time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is considered as the evolution parameter of the ray and the two remaining
 equations are 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\dfrac{d\mathbf{X}}{dt}=-\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\mathbf{k}}/\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\omega}\\
\\\dfrac{d\mathbf{k}}{dt}=\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\mathbf{X}}/\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\omega}\end{array}\label{rayeq1}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 which are the usual forms found in the literature.
 However, one may also keep the simple form
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\dfrac{d\mathbf{X}}{d\tau}=\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\mathbf{k}}\\
\\\dfrac{d\mathbf{k}}{d\tau}=-\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\mathbf{X}}\end{array}\label{rayeq2}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 if the determination of the group velocity from 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial\mathcal{D}/\partial\omega$
\end_inset

 is not required.
 This reduces the number of derivative to calculate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We define 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{Y}$
\end_inset

 the 8-dimension vector that represents the phase-space coordinates
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{Y}=\left(\begin{array}{c}
\mathbf{X}\\
\mathbf{k}\\
t\\
\omega\end{array}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 The ray equations (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "rayeq1"

\end_inset

-
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "rayeq2"

\end_inset

) require to evaluate the derivatives of the dispersion relation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "disprel3"

\end_inset

) with respect to the coordinates of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{Y}$
\end_inset

, explicitly
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial N_{\Vert}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}};\quad\frac{\partial N_{\bot}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}};\quad\frac{\partial X_{ij}^{s}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{X}^{s}=\mathbb{X}^{s}\left(N_{\bot},N_{\Vert},\beta_{Ts},\overline{\omega}_{ps},\overline{\omega}_{cs}\right)$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\frac{\partial X_{ij}^{s}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}} & =\frac{\partial X_{ij}^{s}}{\partial N_{\bot}}\frac{\partial N_{\bot}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}}+\frac{\partial X_{ij}^{s}}{\partial N_{\Vert}}\frac{\partial N_{\Vert}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}}\nonumber \\
 & +\frac{\partial X_{ij}^{s}}{\partial\beta_{Ts}}\frac{\partial\beta_{Ts}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}}+\frac{\partial X_{ij}^{s}}{\partial\overline{\omega}_{ps}}\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{ps}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}}+\frac{\partial X_{ij}^{s}}{\partial\overline{\omega}_{cs}}\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{cs}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}}\label{derivatives}\end{align}

\end_inset

 In this equation the derivatives of the susceptibility tensor depend upon
 the plasma dispersion model (i.e.
 cold, kinetic, etc) while the derivatives with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{Y}$
\end_inset

 are function of the equilibrium and the coordinate system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Canonical-coordinates-in"

\end_inset

Canonical coordinates in an axisymmetric toroidal plasma
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Flux coordinate system
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In an axisymmetric toroidal plasma we define the coordinate system 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\rho,\theta,\phi\right)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is the poloidal angle measured counterclockwise from the horizontal outboard
 midplane, 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 is the toroidal angle measured clockwise from the top, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 is a radial coordinate that varies between 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 on the axis and 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 at the plasma edge.
 The coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\rho\left(\psi\right)$
\end_inset

 must be a flux function, i.e.
 a monotonic function of the poloidal magnetic flux coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 defined by the following expression of the magnetic field 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "dec04a"

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{B}=I\left(\psi\right)\mathbf{\nabla}\phi+\mathbf{\nabla}\phi\times\mathbf{\nabla}\psi\end{equation}

\end_inset

 which can be rewritten as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{B}=\sigma_{B}B_{T}\mathbf{\hat{\phi}}+\sigma_{I}B_{P}\mathbf{\hat{s}}\label{eq:Bfield}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{B}$
\end_inset

 is the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\phi}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{I}$
\end_inset

 is the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{\phi}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{T}=\left\vert I\left(\psi\right)\right\vert /R$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{P}=\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\psi\right\Vert /R$
\end_inset

 are definite positive and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\hat{s}}=\mathbf{\hat{\phi}}\times\mathbf{\hat{\rho}}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\nabla}\rho=\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert \mathbf{\hat{\rho}}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\hat{s}}$
\end_inset

 is orientated counter-clockwise in the poloidal plane.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\rho,\theta,\phi\right)$
\end_inset

 coordinate system the covariant coordinates of the wave vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

 are
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
k_{i}=\left(k_{\rho},m,n\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 The Poisson matrix of canonically conjugate 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{X}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\{ \mathbf{X},\mathbf{k}\right\} =\dfrac{\partial\mathbf{X}}{\partial\mathbf{X}}\cdot\dfrac{\partial\mathbf{k}}{\partial\mathbf{k}}-\dfrac{\partial\mathbf{X}}{\partial\mathbf{k}}\cdot\dfrac{\partial\mathbf{k}}{\partial\mathbf{X}}=\mathbb{I}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Ray equations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the canonically conjugate systems 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\rho,\theta,\phi\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(k_{\rho},m,n\right)$
\end_inset

 the ray equations becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\dfrac{d\rho}{dt}=\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial k_{\rho}}/\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\omega}\\
\\\dfrac{d\theta}{dt}=\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial m}/\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\omega}\\
\\\dfrac{d\phi}{dt}=\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial n}/\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\omega}\end{array}\label{h1}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\dfrac{dk_{\rho}}{dt}=-\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\rho}/\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\omega}\\
\\\dfrac{dm}{dt}=-\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\theta}/\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\omega}\\
\\\dfrac{dn}{dt}=-\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\phi}/\dfrac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\omega}\end{array}\label{h4}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For an axisymmetric magnetic configuration, 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial\mathcal{D}/\partial\phi=0$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is an invariant.
 Therefore, only the first five equations need to be solved.
 The value of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is determined from launching conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Parallel index of refraction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The parallel index of refraction is defined as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
k_{\Vert}=\dfrac{1}{B}\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{B}=\dfrac{1}{B}k_{i}B^{j}\label{kparcalc}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Here 
\begin_inset Formula $B^{j}$
\end_inset

 are the contravariant coordinates of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}$
\end_inset

, given by
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
B^{j}=\mathbf{B}\cdot\mathbf{e}^{j}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{e}^{j}=\left(\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert \mathbf{\hat{\rho}},\dfrac{\mathbf{\hat{\theta}}}{r},\dfrac{\mathbf{\hat{\phi}}}{R}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 such that the contravariant coordinates of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
B^{j}=\left(0,\dfrac{\sigma_{I}B_{P}}{r}\cos\alpha,\dfrac{\sigma_{B}B_{T}}{R}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 is the angle between 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\mathbf{\rho}}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\mathbf{r}}$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\mathbf{\hat{\rho}}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{r}}=\cos\alpha\\
\mathbf{\hat{\rho}}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{\theta}}=-\sin\alpha\\
\mathbf{\hat{s}}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{r}}=\sin\alpha\\
\mathbf{\hat{s}}\cdot\mathbf{\hat{\theta}}=\cos\alpha\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\hat{\rho}}$
\end_inset

 is orientated outward we have 
\begin_inset Formula $-\pi/2<\alpha<\pi/2$
\end_inset

.
 Then (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "kparcalc"

\end_inset

) yields 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\fbox{$k_{\Vert}=\sigma_{I}\cos\alpha P\dfrac{m}{r}+\sigma_{B}T\dfrac{n}{R}$}\label{kpar}\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $P=\dfrac{B_{P}}{B}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $T=\dfrac{B_{T}}{B}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Perpendicular index of refraction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The squared norm of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
k^{2}=g^{ij}k_{i}k_{j}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 which is explicitly
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\fbox{$k^{2}=k_{\rho}^{2}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert ^{2}-2k_{\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert \dfrac{m\sin\alpha}{r}+\dfrac{m^{2}}{r^{2}}+\dfrac{n^{2}}{R^{2}}$}\label{k2}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 From 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{\Vert}$
\end_inset

 the perpendicular wave vector is derived 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
k_{\bot}^{2}=k^{2}-k_{\Vert}^{2}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Rearranging terms yields
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
k_{\bot}^{2}=\left(k_{\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert -\sin\alpha\dfrac{m}{r}\right)^{2}+\left(\sigma_{B}\cos\alpha T\dfrac{m}{r}-\sigma_{I}P\dfrac{n}{R}\right)^{2}\label{kperp2}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Derivatives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the coordinate system is defined we can derive the expressions for
 the derivatives in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "derivatives"

\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{\partial N_{\Vert}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}};\frac{\partial N_{\bot}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}};\frac{\partial\beta_{Ts}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}};\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{ps}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}};\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{cs}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}}\]

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{Y}=\left(\rho,\theta,\phi,k_{\rho},m,n,t,\omega\right)$
\end_inset

.
 The derivatives of the index of refraction are computed using (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "kpar"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "k2"

\end_inset

) using 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{N}=\mathbf{k}c/\omega$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial N_{\bot}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}}=\dfrac{1}{2N_{\bot}}\left(\frac{\partial N^{2}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}}-2N_{\Vert}\frac{\partial N_{\Vert}}{\partial\mathbf{Y}}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 .
 The equilibrium properties are by definition function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\mathbf{X},t\right)$
\end_inset

 only such that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\beta_{Ts}}{\partial\mathbf{k}}=\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{ps}}{\partial\mathbf{k}}=\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{cs}}{\partial\mathbf{k}}=0\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and since 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{Ts}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\beta_{Ts}}{\partial\omega}=0\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Since we consider a constant axisymmetric equilibrium
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\frac{\partial\beta_{Ts}}{\partial t} & =\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{ps}}{\partial t}=\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{cs}}{\partial t}=0\\
\frac{\partial\beta_{Ts}}{\partial\phi} & =\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{ps}}{\partial\phi}=\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{cs}}{\partial\phi}=0\end{align}

\end_inset

 The the temperature and density are flux coordinates such that
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\beta_{Ts}}{\partial\theta}=\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{ps}}{\partial\theta}=0\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and we are left with calculating numerically
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\beta_{Ts}}{\partial\rho};\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{ps}}{\partial\rho};\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{cs}}{\partial\rho};\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{cs}}{\partial\theta};\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{ps}}{\partial\omega};\frac{\partial\overline{\omega}_{cs}}{\partial\omega}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Explicit expressions for these derivatives are found in Appendix 1.2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Specular reflection at the plasma edge
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sometimes a wave is to be reflected within the scrape-off layer, which is
 outside the domain of application of the code.
 In this case, a specular reflection is enforced just before the last closed
 flux-surface at the location 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\rho_{1},\theta_{1},\phi_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 where the incident wave vector is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(k_{\rho1},m_{1},n_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 The condition for the specular reflection is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\mathbf{k}_{2}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf{\rho}}=-\mathbf{k}_{1}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf{\rho}}\\
\mathbf{k}_{2}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf{s}}=\mathbf{k}_{1}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf{s}}\\
\mathbf{k}_{2}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf{\phi}}=\mathbf{k}_{1}\cdot\widehat{\mathbf{\phi}}\end{array}\label{spec}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 with the contravariant basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{e}^{j}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $=\left(\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert \widehat{\mathbf{\rho}},\dfrac{\widehat{\mathbf{\theta}}}{r},\dfrac{\widehat{\mathbf{\phi}}}{R}\right)$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{k}=k_{\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert \widehat{\mathbf{\rho}}+\dfrac{m}{r}\widehat{\mathbf{\theta}}+\dfrac{n}{R}\widehat{\mathbf{\phi}}\label{eq:wavevector}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "spec"

\end_inset

) becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
k_{2\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert -\dfrac{m_{2}}{r}\sin\alpha=-k_{1\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert +\dfrac{m_{1}}{r}\sin\alpha\\
\\\dfrac{m_{2}}{r}\cos\alpha=\dfrac{m_{1}}{r}\cos\alpha\\
\\\dfrac{n_{2}}{R}=\dfrac{n_{1}}{R}\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 are conserved through the reflection and
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
k_{2\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert =2\dfrac{m}{r}\sin\alpha-k_{1\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert \end{equation}

\end_inset

 We should verify that the dispersion relation is satisfied for the outgoing
 wave.
 We have (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "kpar"

\end_inset

), (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "kperp2"

\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
k_{\Vert}=\sigma_{I}P\cos\alpha\dfrac{m}{r}+\sigma_{B}T\dfrac{n}{R}\\
\\k_{\bot}=\sqrt{\left(k_{\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert -\sin\alpha\dfrac{m}{r}\right)^{2}+\left(\sigma_{B}T\cos\alpha\dfrac{m}{r}-\sigma_{I}P\dfrac{n}{R}\right)^{2}}\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
k_{\Vert2}=k_{\Vert1}\\
\\k_{\bot2}=\sqrt{\left(\dfrac{m}{r}\sin\alpha-k_{1\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert \right)^{2}+\left(\sigma_{B}T\cos\alpha\dfrac{m}{r}-\sigma_{I}P\dfrac{n}{R}\right)^{2}}=k_{\bot1}\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and the dispersion relation is satisfied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Inward propagating ray
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "krhoeq"

\end_inset

) for 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{\rho}$
\end_inset

 has two solutions corresponding to inward and outward propagating waves.
 The solution is chosen such that the ray initially propagates inward, meaning
 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{d\rho}{dt}<0\label{inward}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 We have
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{d\rho}{dt}=\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial k_{\rho}}/\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial\omega}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial k_{\rho}}=\frac{\partial N_{\bot}}{\partial k_{\rho}}\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial N_{\bot}}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\Vert}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{\rho}$
\end_inset

.
 For positive energy waves with 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial\mathcal{D}/\partial\omega>0$
\end_inset

 the condition (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "inward"

\end_inset

) thus becomes,
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial N_{\bot}}{\partial k_{\rho}}\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial N_{\bot}}<0\label{inward2}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 We have (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "kperp2"

\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial N_{\bot}}{\partial k_{\rho}}=\dfrac{c\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert }{\omega k_{\bot}}\left(k_{\rho}\left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert -\sin\alpha\dfrac{m}{r}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 In general 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\vert k_{\rho}\right\vert \left\Vert \mathbf{\nabla}\rho\right\Vert >\dfrac{\left\vert m\sin\alpha\right\vert }{r}$
\end_inset

 such that (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "inward2"

\end_inset

) reduces to
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
k_{\rho}\frac{\partial\mathcal{D}}{\partial N_{\bot}}<0\end{equation}

\end_inset

 or equivalently
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\fbox{$k_{\rho}S_{\bot}<0$}\label{inwardcond}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{\bot}$
\end_inset

 is the energy flow density in the perpendicular direction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Numerical-algorithms"

\end_inset

Numerical algorithms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Magnetic equilibrium interpolation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since magnetic equilibrium codes give field components on a discrete two
 dimensional grid mesh 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\psi,\theta\right)$
\end_inset

, it is necessary to perform a numerical interpolation at the ray location.
 This procedure must be fast and very accurate an including first and second
 derivatives upon which the ray-tracing is very sensitive.
 Indeed, coarse interpolation may lead to large cumulative errors along
 the ray path and, ultimately, a wrong trajectory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Among several methods
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that this method is universal, and may be used from any numerical data
 provided from a Grad Shafranov equation solver.
 However, for some codes that use finite elements techniques, it may be
 useful to use those directly instead of performing an additional interpolation
 procedure based on a spline-Fourier expansion.
 Indeed, finite elements contain all data needed for calculating magnetic
 field values at any radial and poloidal position including and first and
 second derivatives with an excellent accuracy.
 The choice to use the spline-Fourier expansion is here justified by the
 need to have a ray-tracing solver as general as possible.
\end_layout

\end_inset

, the combination of Fourier expansion and spline interpolation is chosen,
 taking into account of the periodic nature of the poloidal angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 Any function 
\begin_inset Formula $g\left(\psi,\theta\right)$
\end_inset

 is expressed as a Fourier sum
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
g\left(\psi,\theta\right)=a_{0}\left(\psi\right)+\sum_{n=1}^{n=N}a_{n}^{c}\left(\psi\right)\cos\left(n\theta\right)+a_{n}^{s}\left(\psi\right)\sin\left(n\theta\right)\label{Fourier}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $N\leq N_{\theta}/2$
\end_inset

 according to the Shannon theorem where 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is the number of discrete poloidal values.
 The Fourier 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{n}^{(c,s)}\left(\psi_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 coefficients are determined on each discrete magnetic flux surface 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{i}$
\end_inset

 in order to satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
g\left(\psi_{i},\theta_{j}\right)=a_{0,i}+\sum_{n=1}^{n=N}a_{n,i}^{c}\cos\left(n\theta_{j}\right)+a_{n,i}^{s}\sin\left(n\theta_{j}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 then the coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{n}^{(c,s)}\left(\psi\right)$
\end_inset

 at any radial position 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{i}<\psi<\psi_{i+1}$
\end_inset

 are obtained using a usual cubic spline interpolation
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
a_{n}^{(c,s)}\left(\psi\right)=\alpha_{n,i}^{(c,s)}\psi^{3}+\beta_{n,i}^{(c,s)}\psi^{2}+\gamma_{n,i}^{(c,s)}\psi+\delta_{n,i}^{(c,s)}\label{cubicspline}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 the 3
\begin_inset Formula $^{\text{rd}}$
\end_inset

 order polynomial coefficients being calculated so that at all nodes 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{i},$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{n}^{(c,s)}\left(\psi\right)$
\end_inset

 is continuous, as well as its first and second derivatives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then the derivatives at any 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\psi,\theta\right)$
\end_inset

 position are obtained from the relations
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\dfrac{\partial g\left(\psi,\theta\right)}{\partial\theta}={\displaystyle \sum\limits _{n=1}^{n=N}}n\left[-a_{n}^{c}\left(\psi\right)\sin\left(n\theta\right)+a_{n}^{s}\left(\psi\right)\cos\left(n\theta\right)\right]\\
\\\dfrac{\partial^{2}g\left(\psi,\theta\right)}{\partial\theta^{2}}={\displaystyle \sum\limits _{n=1}^{n=N}}-n^{2}\left[a_{n}^{c}\left(\psi\right)\cos\left(n\theta\right)+a_{n}^{s}\left(\psi\right)\sin\left(n\theta\right)\right]\\
\\\dfrac{\partial g\left(\psi,\theta\right)}{\partial\psi}=\dfrac{da_{0}^{c}\left(\psi\right)}{d\psi}+{\displaystyle \sum\limits _{n=1}^{n=N}}\dfrac{da_{n}^{c}\left(\psi\right)}{d\psi}\cos\left(n\theta\right)+\dfrac{da_{n}^{s}\left(\psi\right)}{d\psi}\sin\left(n\theta\right)\\
\\\dfrac{\partial^{2}g\left(\psi,\theta\right)}{\partial\psi^{2}}=\dfrac{d^{2}a_{0}^{c}\left(\psi\right)}{d\psi^{2}}+{\displaystyle \sum\limits _{n=1}^{n=N}}\dfrac{d^{2}a_{n}^{c}\left(\psi\right)}{d\psi^{2}}\cos\left(n\theta\right)+\dfrac{d^{2}a_{n}^{s}\left(\psi\right)}{d\psi^{2}}\sin\left(n\theta\right)\\
\\\dfrac{\partial^{2}g\left(\psi,\theta\right)}{\partial\psi\partial\theta}=\dfrac{da_{0}^{c}\left(\psi\right)}{d\psi}+{\displaystyle \sum\limits _{n=1}^{n=N}}n\left[-\dfrac{da_{n}^{c}\left(\psi\right)}{d\psi}\sin\left(n\theta\right)+\dfrac{da_{n}^{s}\left(\psi\right)}{d\psi}\cos\left(n\theta\right)\right]\end{array}\label{derivee1_theta}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\dfrac{da_{n}^{(c,s)}\left(\psi\right)}{d\psi}=3\alpha_{n,i}^{(c,s)}\psi^{2}+2\beta_{n,i}^{(c,s)}\psi+\gamma_{n,i}^{(c,s)}\\
\\\dfrac{d^{2}a_{n}^{(c,s)}\left(\psi\right)}{d\psi^{2}}=6\alpha_{n,i}^{(c,s)}\psi+2\beta_{n,i}^{(c,s)}\end{array}\label{derivepsi1}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that for density and temperature profiles only a cubic spline interpolation
 is performed, since these parameters are function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 only.
 With typically 
\begin_inset Formula $65$
\end_inset

 poloidal angle values and 
\begin_inset Formula $101$
\end_inset

 radial discrete positions, the relative accuracy of the magnetic equilibrium
 is of the order of 
\begin_inset Formula $10^{-4}$
\end_inset

 approximately.
 Larger numerical errors may occur near a X-point, where the local curvature
 radius of all magnetic flux surfaces becomes very small.
 In that case and if a more detailed interpolated magnetic reconstruction
 is required, more poloidal points must be used in this region of the plasma,
 keeping the same total number of poloidal values on a non-uniform grid.
 When some smoothing is needed, it is always possible to use less coefficients
 in the Fourier expansion series.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Runge-Kutta differential equation solver
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Relations (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "h1"

\end_inset

-
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "h4"

\end_inset

) form a system of first order coupled differential equations.
 Knowing the initial wave characteristics 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(k_{\psi0},m_{0},n_{0}\right)$
\end_inset

 at the position 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\psi_{0},\theta_{0},\phi_{0}\right),$
\end_inset

 the problem is to evaluate 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(k_{\psi},m,n\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\psi,\theta,\phi\right)$
\end_inset

 at all values of the time parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $t.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are several algorithms for solving this initial value problem, minimizing
 propagation of the numerical error.
 One of the most popular is the Runge-Kutta algorithm which belongs to the
 Euler-Cauchy algorithm family
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
There exists other methods used also for ray-tracing named "predictor-corrector"
 which belong to the general Adams algorithm family.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The procedure consists in solving the differential equations of the type
 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{\prime}\left(x\right)=f\left(x,y\left(x\right)\right)$
\end_inset

 by an iterative method, with the recurrence rule 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
y_{n+1}=y_{n}+h\varphi\left(x_{n},y_{n},h\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and the initial condition 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{0}=y\left(x_{0}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A solution of the following form is seeked 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\varphi\left(x,y,h\right)=\alpha_{1}f\left(x,y\left(x\right)\right)+\alpha_{2}f\left(x+\alpha h,y+\beta hf\left(x,y\right)\right)\label{phiRK}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 so that no numerical derivatives need to be calculated.
 From a Taylor series expansion of 
\begin_inset Formula $y\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 up to the second order, one obtains 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\frac{y\left(x+h\right)-y\left(x\right)}{h} & =y^{\prime}\left(x\right)+\frac{h}{2}y^{\prime\prime}\left(x\right)+O\left(h^{2}\right)\\
 & =f\left(x,y\right)+\frac{h}{2}f^{(1)}\left(x,y\right)+O\left(h^{2}\right)\end{align}

\end_inset

 where
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
f^{(1)}\left(x,y\right)=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}+f\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A similar expansion gives
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
f\left(x+\alpha h,y+\beta hf\left(x,y\right)\right)=f+\alpha h\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}+\beta hf\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}+O\left(h^{2}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Thus, up to the 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{\text{nd }}$
\end_inset

order, 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi$
\end_inset

 becomes
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\varphi\left(x,y,h\right)=\left(\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}\right)f\left(x,y\right)+h\left[\alpha\alpha_{2}\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}+\beta\alpha_{2}f\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right]+O\left(h^{2}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 so that
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\frac{y\left(x+h\right)-y\left(x\right)}{h}-\varphi\left(x,y,h\right) & =\left(1-\alpha_{1}-\alpha_{2}\right)\nonumber \\
 & +h\left(\frac{1}{2}-\alpha\alpha_{2}\right)\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\nonumber \\
 & +h\left(\frac{1}{2}-\beta\alpha_{2}\right)\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}+O\left(h^{2}\right)\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to obtain a second order accurate calculation, the coefficients
 must satisfy the relations
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}=1\\
\alpha\alpha_{2}=1/2\\
\beta\alpha_{2}=1/2\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 which implies
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\alpha=\beta=\dfrac{1}{2\alpha_{2}}\\
\alpha_{1}=1-\alpha_{2}\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The solution is finally 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\varphi\left(x,y,h\right)=\left(1-\alpha\right)f\left(x,y\right)+\alpha f\left(x+\frac{h}{2\alpha},y+\frac{h}{2\alpha}f\left(x,y\right)\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where the case 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=1/2$
\end_inset

 corresponds to the Eun's algorithm, while 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=1$
\end_inset

 is corresponding to the modified Euler's algorithm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A second order Runge-Kutta algorithm then corresponds to the following procedure
,
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{c}
y_{n+1}=y_{n}+h\varphi\left(x_{n},y_{n},h\right)\\
\varphi\left(x_{n},y_{n},h\right)=\left(1-\alpha\right)k_{1}+\alpha k_{2}\\
k_{1}=f\left(x,y\right)\\
k_{2}=f\left(x+\dfrac{h}{2\alpha},y+\dfrac{h}{2\alpha}k_{1}\right)\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 is chosen depending upon the studied problem, and parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $h,$
\end_inset

 which must be small, may be adjusted at each iteration step, as function
 of the local curvature of the ray path.
 This is the adaptative Runge-Kutta method, widely used for solving this
 type of problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This procedure, which has been highlighted for order 
\begin_inset Formula $2,$
\end_inset

 may be expanded in a similar manner to higher orders.
 Detailed calculations can be found in the literature 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pre92"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the ray-tracing code, a sixth order adaptative Runge-Kutta integration
 method is used.
 The numerical accuracy obtained with this method is very satisfactory,
 and the condition 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\vert \mathcal{D}\right\vert \leq10^{-10}$
\end_inset

 is usually obtained along the ray path, despite a sudden increase in 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\vert \mathcal{D}\right\vert $
\end_inset

 sometimes observed near the cold/fast wave mode conversion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Conclusion"

\end_inset

Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The code 
\shape italic
C3PO
\shape default
 has been designed for fast and accurate calculations of the ray trajectories
 in inhomogeneous and anisotropic plasmas with arbitrary axisymmetric magnetic
 equilibrium and nested flux surfaces.
 It is part of the 
\shape italic
C3PO/LUKE/R5-X2
\shape default
 codes package designed for calculating RF current drive in tokamaks or
 reverse field pinches.
 A modular structure makes the code evolutive.
 In particular, additional dielectric response models can be implemented.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Among possible future improvements, 
\shape italic
C3PO
\shape default
 could be developed into a beam tracing code to include diffraction effects
 and calculate the beam size.
 It could also be extended to account for toroidal equilibrium inhomogeneities
 such as the magnetic ripple 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pey96,imb05"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

