\begin_layout Standard
Lower Hybrid heating and current drive simulations have been performed for
 the VERSATOR II and PLT tokamaks, which both have almost circular concentric
 magnetic flux surfaces 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "bon84,bon86"

\end_inset

.
 For the sake of benchmarking, the Shafranov shift is neglected, and a plasma
 with exact circular concentric magnetic flux surfaces is considered with
 the following magnetic equilibrium parameters 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline   &  VERSATOR$\text{ II}$  &  PLT\\
\hline $R_{p}$  &  $0.4\text{ m}$  &  $1.32\text{ m}$\\
\hline $a_{p}$  &  $0.13\text{ m}$  &  $0.4\text{ m}$\\
\hline $\hat{B}_{T}$  &  $1.4\text{ T}$  &  $3.1\text{ T}$\\
\hline $I_{p}$  &  $0.05\text{ MA}$  &  $0.2\text{ MA}$\\\hline \end{tabular}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and the following profiles 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
n_{e}\left(\rho\right)=\left(n_{e0}-n_{ea}\right)\frac{e^{\alpha_{n}}-e^{\alpha_{n}\rho^{2}}}{e^{\alpha_{n}}-1}+n_{ea}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 with
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline   &  VERSATOR$\text{ II}$  &  PLT\\
\hline $n_{e0}$  &  $2.0\times10^{19}\text{ m}^{-3}$  &  $0.5625\times10^{19}\text{ m}^{-3}$\\
\hline $n_{ea}$  &  $2.0\times10^{18}\text{ m}^{-3}$  &  $0.5625\times10^{18}\text{ m}^{-3}$\\
\hline $\alpha_{n}$  &  $0.1$  &  $-0.571$\\\hline \end{tabular}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the calculations, the scrape-off layer is not considered, since the specular
 reflection is enforced if needed on the last closed magnetic surface for
 the Lower Hybrid wave.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For both tokamaks, 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{\text{eff}}$
\end_inset

 is uniform with 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{\text{eff}}=4$
\end_inset

 for VERSATOR II and 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{\text{eff}}=2$
\end_inset

 for PLT.
 The plasma is made of hydrogen ions for VERSATOR II and deuterium ions
 for PLT.
 For both machines, a fully stripped single impurity species is considered,
 using carbon ions with 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{c}=12,$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{c}=6.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The magnetic equilibrium is characterized by a parabolic safety factor profile
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
q\left(\rho\right)=q_{\min}+\left(q_{\max}-q_{\min}\right)\rho^{2}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 The poloidal flux coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 is then 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\psi=\frac{1}{2}\frac{\hat{B}_{T}a_{p}^{2}}{q_{\max}-q_{\min}}\ln\left[\frac{q\left(\rho\right)}{q_{\min}}\right]\end{equation}

\end_inset

 which leads to
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\psi_{a}=\frac{1}{2}\frac{\hat{B}_{T}a_{p}^{2}}{q_{\max}-q_{\min}}\ln\left[\frac{q_{\max}}{q_{\min}}\right]\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the simulations, 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{\min}=1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{\max}$
\end_inset

 is obtained from Ampère's law.
\end_layout

