\begin_layout Standard
In vacuum, electromagnetic waves trajectories are straight lines, which
 offers an interesting benchmarking case for the curvilinear coordinate
 system used with 
\begin_inset Formula $C3PO$
\end_inset

 ray-tracing.
 It is enforced that at 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi=\psi_{a}$
\end_inset

 the wave is reflected like in a mirror, so that the ray remains confined
 in the machine.
 Circular concentric poloidal magnetic flux surfaces are considered with
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline  $R_{p}$  &  $3.0\text{ m}$\\
\hline $a_{p}$  &  $0.9\text{ m}$\\
\hline $\hat{B}_{T}$  &  $3.0\text{ T}$\\\hline \end{tabular}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and the poloidal magnetic field is considered to be created by a virtual
 toroidal current 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{p}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $1.0$
\end_inset

 MA and a parabolic safety factor profile of the form
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
q\left(\rho\right)=q_{\min}+\left(q_{\max}-q_{\min}\right)\rho^{2}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{\min}=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{\max}$
\end_inset

 obtained from Ampère's law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since rays are straight lines, it is possible to calculate the exact rays
 trajectories from geometrical arguments.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert \nabla\rho\right\Vert =1$
\end_inset

, the initial value of the wave vector is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}_{0}=k_{\rho0}\widehat{\rho}+k_{\theta0}\widehat{\theta}+k_{\phi0}\widehat{\phi}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{\theta0}=m_{0}a_{p}/\rho_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{\phi}=n_{0}a_{p}/R_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}_{0}$
\end_inset

 is expressed in cartesian coordinates using the relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}_{0}^{cartesian}=\mathbb{M}_{cc}\left(\theta_{0},\phi_{0}\right)\cdot\mathbf{k}_{0}^{curvilinear}$
\end_inset

 where
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbb{M}_{cc}\left(\theta,\phi\right)=\left(\begin{array}{ccc}
\cos\theta\cos\phi & -\sin\theta\cos\phi & -\sin\phi\\
\cos\theta\sin\phi & -\sin\theta\sin\phi & \cos\phi\\
\sin\theta & \cos\theta & 0\end{array}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 The initial position at launch in the cartesian coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\overline{x}_{0},\overline{y}_{0},\overline{z}_{0}\right)$
\end_inset

 normalized to 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{p}$
\end_inset

 is given by the relations
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\overline{x}_{0}=\left(\dfrac{R_{p}}{a_{p}}+\rho_{0}\cos\theta_{0}\right)\cos\phi_{0}\\
\overline{y}_{0}=\left(\dfrac{R_{p}}{a_{p}}+\rho_{0}\cos\theta_{0}\right)\sin\phi_{0}\\
\overline{z}_{0}=\rho_{0}\sin\theta_{0}\end{array}\label{xyz}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\rho_{0},\theta_{0},\phi_{0}\right)$
\end_inset

 are initial curvilinear coordinates.
 Since the ray is a straight line, its position is given by the parametric
 equation
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\overline{x}^{\left(l\right)}=\overline{x}_{0}+k_{x0}s\left(l\right)/k_{0}\\
\overline{y}^{\left(l\right)}=\overline{y}_{0}+k_{y0}s\left(l\right)/k_{0}\\
\overline{z}^{\left(l\right)}=\overline{z}_{0}+k_{z0}s\left(l\right)/k_{0}\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $s\left(l\right)$
\end_inset

 is the ray length starting from 
\begin_inset Formula $s\left(l=0\right)=0$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\overline{x}_{0},\overline{y}_{0},\overline{z}_{0}\right).$
\end_inset

Once 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\overline{x}^{\left(l\right)},\overline{y}^{\left(l\right)},\overline{z}^{\left(l\right)}\right)$
\end_inset

 known, it is possible to determine back 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\rho,\theta,\phi\right)$
\end_inset

 by inverting (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "xyz"

\end_inset

).
 From the determination of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\rho,\theta,\phi\right),$
\end_inset

 it is possible to evaluate 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}_{l}^{curvilinear}$
\end_inset

at all positions, using 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}_{l}^{cartesian}=\mathbf{k}_{0}^{cartesian}$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{k}_{l}^{curvilinear}=\mathbb{M}_{cc}^{-1}\left(\theta,\phi\right)\cdot\mathbf{k}_{0}^{cartesian}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $m^{\left(l\right)}=k_{\theta}^{\left(l\right)}\rho^{\left(l\right)}/a_{p}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n^{\left(l\right)}=k_{\phi}^{\left(l\right)}R^{\left(l\right)}/a_{p}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to keep the ray inside the toroidal chamber, a specular reflection
 is enforced at 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{a}.$
\end_inset

If the condition 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho^{\left(l\right)}>1$
\end_inset

 is encountered, then the new direction of the ray 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}_{0\left(i+1\right)}^{cartesian}$
\end_inset

 of the 
\begin_inset Formula $i+1$
\end_inset

 ray segment is deduced from 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}_{0\left(i\right)}^{cartesian}$
\end_inset

 according to the conditions 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\mathbf{k}_{0\left(i+1\right)}^{cartesian}\cdot\widehat{\rho}^{\left(l-1\right)} & =-\mathbf{k}_{0\left(i\right)}^{cartesian}\cdot\widehat{\rho}^{\left(l-1\right)}\\
\mathbf{k}_{0\left(i+1\right)}^{cartesian}\cdot\widehat{\theta}^{\left(l-1\right)} & =\mathbf{k}_{0\left(i\right)}^{cartesian}\cdot\widehat{\theta}^{\left(l-1\right)}\\
\mathbf{k}_{0\left(i+1\right)}^{cartesian}\cdot\widehat{\phi}^{\left(l-1\right)} & =\mathbf{k}_{0\left(i\right)}^{cartesian}\cdot\widehat{\phi}^{\left(l-1\right)}\end{align}

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\widehat{\rho},\widehat{\theta},\widehat{\phi}\right)$
\end_inset

 are used at the increment 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(l-1\right).$
\end_inset

 It corresponds to the relation
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{k}_{0\left(i+1\right)}^{cartesian}=\left[\mathbb{M}_{cc1}^{-1}\left(\theta^{\left(l-1\right)},\phi^{\left(l-1\right)}\right)\cdot\mathbb{M}_{cc2}\left(\theta^{\left(l-1\right)},\phi^{\left(l-1\right)}\right)\right]\cdot\mathbf{k}_{0\left(i\right)}^{cartesian}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 with
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbb{M}_{cc1}\left(\theta,\phi\right)=\left(\begin{array}{ccc}
\cos\theta\cos\phi & \cos\theta\sin\phi & \sin\theta\\
-\sin\theta\cos\phi & -\sin\theta\sin\phi & \cos\theta\\
\sin\phi & -\cos\phi & 0\end{array}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbb{M}_{cc2}\left(\theta,\phi\right)=\left(\begin{array}{ccc}
-\cos\theta\cos\phi & -\cos\theta\sin\phi & -\sin\theta\\
-\sin\theta\cos\phi & -\sin\theta\sin\phi & \cos\theta\\
\sin\phi & -\cos\phi & 0\end{array}\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Wave initial conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the example here given, the ray is launched from the position
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\rho_{0}=0.98\\
\theta_{0}=\pi/4\\
\phi_{0}=0\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 with spectral properties
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline  $\omega/\left(2\pi\right)$  &  $530.15\text{ GHz}$\\
\hline $N_{\Vert0}$  &  $0.5$\\
\hline $m_{0}$  &  $-3000$\\\hline \end{tabular}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 so that the wave can propagate into vacuum region.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As expected, ray trajectory is made of pieces of straight lines, which can
 be easily shown from the toroidal projection in Fig 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "FigVact"

\end_inset

.
 Because of the toroidal topology, the poloidal projection in Fig.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "FigVacp"

\end_inset

 does not give straight lines.
 This result highlights the fact that it is very difficult to have a correct
 picture of the ray path from this type of representation.
 Comparison with simple geometrical optics is excellent.
\end_layout

