\begin_layout Standard
In Reverse Field Pinch, the plasma evolves to a quasi-stationary magnetic
 equilibrium close to the force free minimum energy states described by
 Taylor 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "tay74"

\end_inset

.
 The fully relaxed Taylor states are characterized by
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\nabla\times\mathbf{B}=\lambda\mathbf{B}\label{RFPequilibrium}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 with
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\lambda=\mu\mathbf{J}\cdot\mathbf{B}/B^{2}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 constant throughout the plasma.
 A usual simplified approach is to consider a circular concentric toroidal
 plasma such that
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\rho=\dfrac{r}{a_{p}}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 with the following equilibrium parameters
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Parameters are corresponding to the large RFP machine of Madison University
 (USA)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline  $R_{p}$  &  $1.5\text{ m}$\\
\hline $a_{p}$  &  $0.5\text{ m}$\\
\hline $\hat{B}_{T}$  &  $0.2\text{ T}$\\
\hline $I_{p}$  &  $0.3\text{ MA}$\\\hline \end{tabular}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "RFPequilibrium"

\end_inset

, considering a constant 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 value, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\left(\nabla\times\mathbf{B}\right)_{r} & =\frac{1}{rR}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\left(RB_{\phi}\right)-\frac{1}{R}\frac{\partial}{\partial\phi}\left(RB_{\theta}\right)\\
\left(\nabla\times\mathbf{B}\right)_{\theta} & =-\frac{1}{R}\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\left(RB_{\phi}\right)\\
\left(\nabla\times\mathbf{B}\right)_{\phi} & =\frac{1}{r}\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\left(rB_{\theta}\right)\end{align}

\end_inset

 which leads to two coupled equations
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
-\frac{1}{R}\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\left(RB_{\phi}\right) & =\lambda B_{\theta}\label{RFPequiltheta}\\
\frac{1}{r}\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\left(rB_{\theta}\right) & =\lambda B_{\phi}\label{RFPequilphi}\end{align}

\end_inset

 Inserting Eq.
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "RFPequiltheta"

\end_inset

 into Eq.
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "RFPequilphi"

\end_inset

 in order to eliminate 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\theta},$
\end_inset

 one finds the equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\phi}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{1}{r}\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\left(\frac{r}{R}\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\left(RB_{\phi}\right)\right)=-\lambda^{2}B_{\phi}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 which can be developed as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial r^{2}}\left(RB_{\phi}\right)+\frac{R}{r}\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\left(\frac{r}{R}\right)\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\left(RB_{\phi}\right)+\lambda^{2}RB_{\phi}=0\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming a low inverse aspect ratio limit, and neglecting all terms higher
 than 
\begin_inset Formula $(a_{p}/R_{p})^{2},$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\left(\frac{r}{R}\right)\simeq\frac{1}{R}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial r^{2}}\left(RB_{\phi}\right)+\frac{1}{r}\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\left(RB_{\phi}\right)+\lambda^{2}RB_{\phi}=0\end{equation}

\end_inset

 which is a Bessel equation whose solution is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
RB_{\phi}=J_{0}\left(\lambda r\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Reporting this equation in Eq.
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "RFPequiltheta"

\end_inset

, and using the derivative identity for Bessel functions of order 
\begin_inset Formula $m,$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{d}{dx}\left(x^{m}J_{m}\left(x\right)\right)=x^{m}J_{m-1}\left(x\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset

 one finds
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
RB_{\theta}=-J_{-1}\left(\lambda r\right)=J_{+1}\left(\lambda r\right)\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From the determination of 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\phi}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\theta},$
\end_inset

 it turns finally out that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\nabla\times\mathbf{B}\right)_{r}=0$
\end_inset

, and therefore Eq.
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "RFPequilibrium"

\end_inset

 is well satisfied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the ray-tracing calculations, the magnetic equilibrium is chosen so
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda a_{p}=\widetilde{\lambda}=2.2.$
\end_inset

 From these definition, up to the same order, the safety factor is
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\fbox{$q\simeq\dfrac{a_{p}}{R_{p}}\rho\dfrac{J_{0}\left(\widetilde{\lambda}\rho\right)}{J_{1}\left(\widetilde{\lambda}\rho\right)}$}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the cold plasma model, the electron density profile is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
n_{e}\left(\rho\right)=\left(n_{e0}-n_{ea}\right)\left(1-\rho^{2}\right)^{\alpha_{n_{e}}}+n_{ea}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 with
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
n_{e0}=1.4\times10^{19}\text{ m}^{-3}\\
n_{ea}=0.24\times n_{e0}\\
\alpha_{n_{e}}=1.3\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 while the main plasma gas is Deuterium.
 Calculations are performed with a uniform effective charge 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{eff}=2$
\end_inset

, using the fully stripped single impurity model, with Carbon.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Wave initial conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The ray is launched in the slow mode from the position
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\rho_{0}=0.968\\
\theta_{0}=3\pi/4\\
\phi_{0}=0\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 with the spectral properties
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\omega/\left(2\pi\right)=0.8\text{ GHz}\\
N_{\Vert0}=10.0\\
n_{0}=0\end{array}\end{equation}

\end_inset

 It is worth noting that since at the plasma edge of a RFP, magnetic field
 lines are almost in the poloidal direction, the toroidal wave number 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{0}$
\end_inset

 must be zero, instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{0}$
\end_inset

 usually used for tokamaks, where edge magnetic field lines are almost aligned
 toroidally.
 Because of the axisymmetry, 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 remains equal to zero along the ray propagation.
 Nevertheless, rays may propagate toroidally.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results are shown in figures 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "FigRFPp"

\end_inset

-
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "FigRFPd"

\end_inset

.
 A strong poloidal upshift is observed as the LH wave propagates, and as
 found by previous publications, the ray does a spiral around the magnetic
 axis.
 Even if the magnetic equilibrium strongly differs from the usual tokamak
 one, the dispersion relation is well fulfilled along the ray path.
\end_layout

